# LeetCode 128 — Longest Consecutive Sequence

Pattern: HashSet + Consecutive Sequence

Question: Given an unsorted integer array, find the length of the longest consecutive sequence. The numbers array mein consecutive hone zaroori nahi hain, aur solution \\(O(n)\\) time mein hona chahiye.

Example:

- Input: `nums = [100, 4, 200, 1, 3, 2]`
- Output: `4`
- Explanation: Longest consecutive sequence `1, 2, 3, 4` hai. Iski length `4` hai.

## 1. Brute Force — Har sequence ko search karo

Logic:

1. Har number ko sequence ka starting point maano.
2. Baar-baar check karo ki next number (`num + 1`) array mein present hai ya nahi.
3. Har sequence ki length count karo aur maximum return karo.

```
class Solution {
    public int longestConsecutive(int[] nums) {
        int longest = 0;

        for (int num : nums) {
            int current = num;
            int count = 1;

            while (contains(nums, current + 1)) {
                current++;
                count++;
            }

            longest = Math.max(longest, count);
        }

        return longest;
    }

    // Array mein target number search karo
    private boolean contains(int[] nums, int target) {
        for (int num : nums) {
            if (num == target) {
                return true;
            }
        }

        return false;
    }
}
```

- Time: \\(O(n^3)\\) worst case
- Space: \\(O(1)\\) extra space

## 2. Better — Sorting

Logic:

1. Array ko sort karo.
2. Agar current number, previous number se `1` bada hai, to sequence length badhao.
3. Duplicate numbers ko ignore karo.
4. Maximum length return karo.

```
import java.util.Arrays;

class Solution {
    public int longestConsecutive(int[] nums) {
        if (nums.length == 0) {
            return 0;
        }

        Arrays.sort(nums);

        int longest = 1;
        int count = 1;

        for (int i = 1; i < nums.length; i++) {
            if (nums[i] == nums[i - 1]) {
                // Duplicate number ko ignore karo
                continue;
            }

            if (nums[i] == nums[i - 1] + 1) {
                count++;
            } else {
                // Sequence break ho gayi
                count = 1;
            }

            longest = Math.max(longest, count);
        }

        return longest;
    }
}
```

- Time: \\(O(n\log n)\\)
- Space: \\(O(\log n)\\) auxiliary space for typical Java primitive-array sorting implementation.

## 3. Optimal — HashSet

Logic:

1. Saare numbers ko `HashSet` mein daalo, taaki lookup average \\(O(1)\\) ho.
2. Sirf us number se sequence start karo jiska previous number (`num - 1`) set mein nahi hai.
3. `num + 1`, `num + 2`... check karke sequence length count karo.
4. Maximum length return karo.

Important trick: Sequence ka starting number wahi hai jiske just pehle wala number set mein nahi hai.

```
import java.util.HashSet;
import java.util.Set;

class Solution {
    public int longestConsecutive(int[] nums) {
        Set<Integer> set = new HashSet<>();

        // Step 1: Saare numbers HashSet mein daalo
        for (int num : nums) {
            set.add(num);
        }

        int longest = 0;

        // Step 2: Har number ko check karo
        for (int num : set) {

            // Sequence tabhi start hogi jab previous number na ho
            if (!set.contains(num - 1)) {
                int current = num;
                int count = 1;

                // Step 3: Consecutive numbers search karo
                while (set.contains(current + 1)) {
                    current++;
                    count++;
                }

                longest = Math.max(longest, count);
            }
        }

        return longest;
    }
}
```

- Time: \\(O(n)\\) average
- Space: \\(O(n)\\)

## Dry Run

Input: `nums = [100, 4, 200, 1, 3, 2]`

HashSet: `{100, 4, 200, 1, 3, 2}`

| Number | Previous exists? | Action                      |
| ------ | ---------------- | --------------------------- |
| 100    | No               | Sequence length = 1         |
| 4      | Yes (`3`)        | Start nahi karenge          |
| 200    | No               | Sequence length = 1         |
| 1      | No               | `1 → 2 → 3 → 4`, length = 4 |
| 3      | Yes (`2`)        | Start nahi karenge          |
| 2      | Yes (`1`)        | Start nahi karenge          |

Output: `4`

## Comparison

| Approach    | Time               | Extra Space     |
| ----------- | ------------------ | --------------- |
| Brute Force | \\(O(n^3)\\)       | \\(O(1)\\)      |
| Sorting     | \\(O(n\log n)\\)   | \\(O(\log n)\\) |
| HashSet     | \\(O(n)\\) average | \\(O(n)\\)      |

Yaad rakhne ki line: `HashSet` banao → previous number absent ho tabhi sequence start karo → next consecutive numbers count karo.
